# Overview
#
# Each phrase announces that the writer is about to say a thing again,
# so the reader reads one point twice. This tier reports `In short,` and
# `Ultimately,` as sentence openers that announce a summary. The fix
# makes the first statement stand alone and deletes the phrase. The rule
# sits at Recommended, because each opener also leads a point that the
# paragraph has not made yet.
#
# Expression
#
# Each phrase counts only with the glued comma of an opener.
#
# False Positives
#
# Without the comma, `ultimately` sits in the middle of a sentence and
# places a step last (`the request ultimately reaches the scheduler`).
#
# Related Rules
#
# The Required ProseRestatement holds every other restatement.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 13 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 5 of 13 sampled
# hits announced a summary of a point that the text had made. In 7
# misfires, the opener led a point that the paragraph had not made yet
# (`Ultimately, your applications run as containers inside Pods`). In
# the other misfire, `ultimately,` sat between two commas in the middle
# of a sentence (`and so, ultimately, hold different types`).
expr main [(in short,), (ultimately,)]
let message "Restatement. Make the first statement stand alone, and delete the phrase."
let description "Flags `in short` and `ultimately` as sentence openers that announce a summary."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "The request ultimately reaches the scheduler."
